WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.446 --> 00:00:03.227
Hello!

00:00:03.227 --> 00:00:05.610
Math Experiments Channel.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:10.000
Today, we will continue exploring approximations of the function Sin[x].

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.675
More precisely, I will do the exploration and you will watch.

00:00:12.675 --> 00:00:17.138
But I hope you're also explore something on your own.

00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:20.646
I have this problem: once I start an exploration, I can't stop.

00:00:20.646 --> 00:00:23.828
I keep explore and explore further...

00:00:23.828 --> 00:00:29.561
Research directions begin to branch out and deepen,

00:00:29.561 --> 00:00:32.577
and it's just impossible to stop.

00:00:32.577 --> 00:00:37.281
But that's not a problem. In fact, I recommend never stopping.

00:00:37.281 --> 00:00:42.529
When you take those final steps, you find the most interesting moments.

00:00:42.529 --> 00:00:49.149
So, patience to take those final steps is a crucial thing.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:52.236
Today, I hope to reach the zeta function.

00:00:52.236 --> 00:01:01.319
That means we'll start approximating Sin[x] and reach the zeta function.

00:01:01.319 --> 00:01:06.286
The distance from the sine function to the zeta function is not that great.

00:01:06.286 --> 00:01:09.200
Let's see how it will go.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.718
Today's material is challenging.

00:01:12.718 --> 00:01:15.037
With two stars.

00:01:15.037 --> 00:01:20.000
Although it's hard to explain what exactly makes this material challenging.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.900
I'm not doing any integrals here.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.912
I'm not doing any complex mathematical calculations.

00:01:26.912 --> 00:01:32.382
But the code itself probably looks complex.

00:01:32.382 --> 00:01:36.148
And also, of course, there's this intuition,

00:01:36.148 --> 00:01:41.362
when you need to see a component in the approximation that can be subtracted.

00:01:41.362 --> 00:01:46.725
You need to visually see sixth-degree polynomials or power functions,

00:01:46.725 --> 00:01:52.028
or see that you have (x^2) * Sin[x] in front of you.

00:01:52.028 --> 00:01:58.088
So, some experience with functions is necessary.

00:01:58.088 --> 00:02:00.951
But experience comes with practice.

00:02:00.951 --> 00:02:04.009
Let's see how it goes.

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:05.253
The idea was as follows:

00:02:05.253 --> 00:02:09.017
Take the function 1/Sin[x].

00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:10.889
And it looks like this.

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:15.069
These pieces resemble hyperbolas.

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:18.591
If we change the scale a bit,

00:02:18.591 --> 00:02:19.560
it rises up

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.531
and here returns from minus infinity. Then again...

00:02:23.531 --> 00:02:32.686
The idea is simple: let's try to express the sum of hyperbolas living in these points.

00:02:32.686 --> 00:02:34.438
Maybe something will work out.

00:02:34.438 --> 00:02:36.383
And indeed, it does.

00:02:36.383 --> 00:02:38.197
Hyperbolas are called poles.

00:02:38.197 --> 00:02:43.295
Places where we have asymptotes going up and down.

00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:46.659
These numerators of the poles are called residues.

00:02:46.659 --> 00:02:52.326
Essentially, we found the residues of the function 1/Sin[x]

00:02:52.326 --> 00:02:56.286
and tried to sum a certain number of residues in the vicinity of zero

00:02:56.286 --> 00:02:58.242
and compare the results.

00:02:58.242 --> 00:03:04.829
Here, I'm comparing Sin[x] with 1 divided by the sum of residues.

00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:08.657
And we see that the functions almost coincide in the vicinity of small "x".

00:03:08.657 --> 00:03:14.149
Well, then, of course, there are significant divergences.

00:03:14.149 --> 00:03:17.862
The blue plot goes up and down,

00:03:17.862 --> 00:03:24.152
while Sin[x] calmly oscillates around zero.

00:03:24.152 --> 00:03:35.293
A very natural idea is to look at the difference of the function to which we approximate our function Sin[x].

00:03:35.293 --> 00:03:38.406
Here we have something similar to xSin[x].

00:03:38.406 --> 00:03:41.632
Not exactly xSin[x], of course.

00:03:41.632 --> 00:03:48.506
xSin[x] looks different.

00:03:48.506 --> 00:03:52.816
Firstly, it becomes very large here. We need to multiply it by something.

00:04:00.698 --> 00:04:01.786
There is some difference.

00:04:01.786 --> 00:04:05.779
It is visible that our xSin[x] is oscillating around zero,

00:04:05.779 --> 00:04:08.413
while here, you see, it is a skewed xSin[x].

00:04:08.413 --> 00:04:10.730
And the oscillations of the blue plot are twice as frequent.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.835
In short, what we did here:

00:04:12.835 --> 00:04:26.252
we divided this plot, the blue one, by "x", after which it started to resemble a regular oscillatory plot.

00:04:26.252 --> 00:04:31.691
But we need to understand the scale here.

00:04:31.691 --> 00:04:35.913
And we need to understand that the oscillations occur twice as often.

00:04:35.913 --> 00:04:38.067
Well, we figured that out.

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:40.669
To restore the scale to normal,

00:04:40.669 --> 00:04:46.676
from 5 * 10^-6 to normal numbers in the range from 0 to 1,

00:04:46.676 --> 00:04:52.390
we had to multiply it by 20m^2.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:58.329
And if we try different values of m here,

00:04:58.329 --> 00:05:00.504
we will get small numbers.

00:05:00.504 --> 00:05:02.552
Here we have taken the next step.

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:08.482
Here, after this event, we found Cos[2x],

00:05:08.482 --> 00:05:12.803
subtracted it, and this is the result of the subtraction.

00:05:12.803 --> 00:05:16.135
And here we went down the wrong path,

00:05:16.135 --> 00:05:20.219
when we started studying this thing.

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:24.967
We should have studied another thing.

00:05:24.967 --> 00:05:30.000
We should have simply multiplied this expression by g[x, m].

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.327
Well, what can we do...

00:05:32.327 --> 00:05:34.103
We took Sin[x],

00:05:34.103 --> 00:05:35.052
took the approximation,

00:05:35.052 --> 00:05:36.734
subtracted one from the other,

00:05:36.734 --> 00:05:38.207
didn't think for long...

00:05:38.207 --> 00:05:42.692
But then we realized that it would have been better to act this way.

00:05:42.692 --> 00:05:47.403
But I want to deliberately go down the wrong path for now,

00:05:47.403 --> 00:05:54.647
to hit my head against something and understand... see the wrongness of this path.

00:05:54.647 --> 00:06:00.227
And for now, we have this

00:06:00.227 --> 00:06:02.065
and we wonder what this 20 is.

00:06:02.065 --> 00:06:06.375
It could be 2Pi^2, or it could be a solid 20.

00:06:06.375 --> 00:06:15.127
If we assume that we prefer the yellow plot over the blue one, then we should go with 2Pi^2.

00:06:15.127 --> 00:06:19.390
Here it's unclear, although the picture is undoubtedly beautiful.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:25.232
If we need to go with 2Pi^2, here's what we have.

00:06:25.232 --> 00:06:27.594
Such an approximation.

00:06:27.594 --> 00:06:34.320
Or if we replace it with Cos[2x-1]/2 multiplied by Sin[x]^2, then this is what we have.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:37.754
Alright. Let's move on.

00:06:37.754 --> 00:06:39.057
Here...

00:06:39.057 --> 00:06:40.000
I won't repeat.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.777
Also, in the previous video, we took a few steps

00:06:42.777 --> 00:06:46.573
and figured out to replace m^2 with m(m+1).

00:06:46.573 --> 00:06:50.377
It's quite a difficult guess,

00:06:50.377 --> 00:06:54.917
which certainly makes the subsequent steps much easier.

00:06:54.917 --> 00:06:58.855
But in reality, it also leads to a dead end.

00:06:58.855 --> 00:07:07.067
So at some point, we have to abandon m(m+1) and go back to m^2.

00:07:07.067 --> 00:07:10.632
It makes you want to bang your head against the wall again.

00:07:10.632 --> 00:07:13.460
This is what we have here.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:18.120
At this stage, I see an oscillatory function here.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:21.160
Although around zero, everything is somehow messed up

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.850
and it doesn't get fixed by increasing m.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:27.809
But I want to repeat these oscillations as much as possible

00:07:27.809 --> 00:07:31.462
And here comes the idea: what if it's Sin[x]^2 again?

00:07:31.462 --> 00:07:33.801
And yes! It looks like it.

00:07:33.801 --> 00:07:36.928
Although... how can I say.

00:07:36.928 --> 00:07:42.157
Now I'm not absolutely certain if it's exactly 1.

00:07:42.157 --> 00:07:46.160
Maybe it's 0.95 here, who knows.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:49.266
We found the yellow plot that matches the blue one in some places,

00:07:49.266 --> 00:07:53.620
and then we subtract the yellow plot from the blue one.

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:57.010
Well, I'll use these words...

00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:00.000
Of course, functions are subtract.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.189
All of this goes off to infinity

00:08:02.189 --> 00:08:12.159
and, of course, we want to multiply this difference by x.

00:08:12.159 --> 00:08:19.058
Maybe even multiply it by x again.

00:08:19.058 --> 00:08:21.441
Alright... Good.

00:08:25.711 --> 00:08:29.400
Here, it's already something unclear...

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:32.005
Next, we try to draw these things.

00:08:32.005 --> 00:08:33.653
Well... good.

00:08:33.653 --> 00:08:35.511
Here's our approximation.

00:08:35.511 --> 00:08:37.850
Here's the function we're approximating.

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:41.470
And then various ideas for remainders come into play.

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:47.093
The remainders already have m in the denominator.

00:08:47.093 --> 00:08:49.651
So, as m increases

00:08:49.651 --> 00:08:51.695
it tends towards this,

00:08:51.695 --> 00:08:56.848
but we're interested in the remainders.

00:08:56.848 --> 00:09:01.718
The colors in Mathematica go as follows: blue, yellow, green, red, violet.

00:09:01.718 --> 00:09:05.597
It's impossible to understand, you just need to memorize.

00:09:05.597 --> 00:09:08.211
And what I'm trying here.

00:09:08.211 --> 00:09:10.175
The blue plot - I simply take m^2.

00:09:10.175 --> 00:09:14.966
And here's this guess that m^2 should be replaced with some second-degree polynomial.

00:09:14.966 --> 00:09:21.382
And by trial and error, it was found to be m(m+1).

00:09:21.382 --> 00:09:23.922
Maybe take a different interval after all.

00:09:23.922 --> 00:09:26.655
For example, like this.

00:09:26.655 --> 00:09:28.688
Or even larger,

00:09:28.688 --> 00:09:34.705
to see the difference between all these ol.

00:09:34.705 --> 00:09:38.032
The blue plot is hidden behind the green one.

00:09:38.032 --> 00:09:41.816
I know that and I'm telling you.

00:09:41.816 --> 00:09:46.618
The yellow plot (see?) is much better than the blue-green one.

00:09:46.618 --> 00:09:52.082
And the red and violet ones don't provide any significant improvements compared to the yellow plot.

00:09:52.082 --> 00:09:54.290
So there is the yellow plot

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:57.954
and here are attempts to guess the value of the second term.

00:09:57.954 --> 00:10:01.265
This is the first term, an approximation of the difference.

00:10:01.265 --> 00:10:03.922
This is the second term.

00:10:03.922 --> 00:10:08.823
But all of them are unsuccessful.

00:10:08.823 --> 00:10:12.323
So you don't have to look at them carefully.

00:10:12.323 --> 00:10:16.988
But this is a process that is important to show, how it all happens.

00:10:16.988 --> 00:10:18.764
One must admit.

00:10:18.764 --> 00:10:23.532
Yes. The yellow plot was an improvement over the blue-green one,

00:10:23.532 --> 00:10:29.852
but the violet and red, although we saw something there,

00:10:29.852 --> 00:10:32.335
it didn't help much.

00:10:32.335 --> 00:10:40.000
We saw this sine wave far from x = 0.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.600
In the range of large x values, the violet plot may be good.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:46.914
Yes, it's good. See?

00:10:46.914 --> 00:10:52.664
The violet plot is closer to the x-axis compared to the red one,

00:10:52.664 --> 00:10:58.070
but not "dramatically," as the English say.

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:02.367
Attempts to add one more term by analogy also don't yield anything.

00:11:02.367 --> 00:11:05.976
There is green plot...

00:11:05.976 --> 00:11:08.403
Here it is slightly better than the yellow one,

00:11:08.403 --> 00:11:11.585
and here the opposite.

00:11:11.585 --> 00:11:13.191
And all of this is just the beginning.

00:11:13.191 --> 00:11:20.000
The beginning of this game of subtraction and division.

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:24.730
Let's engage in it more carefully.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.318
As Zhvanetsky said: "You need to be more thorough, more thorough."

00:11:27.318 --> 00:11:32.903
Now we will try to do all this "more thoroughly."

00:11:32.903 --> 00:11:40.000
And here I have come to such a tangle.

00:11:41.576 --> 00:11:43.439
Which...

00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:46.900
Depending on the scale, we see different pictures.

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:49.550
It's a power function.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:55.874
If we slightly decrease the scale, we see a sine wave above of the power function.

00:11:55.874 --> 00:11:58.853
The power function is not just any parabola like x^2,

00:11:58.853 --> 00:12:06.239
but x^6 or something like that.

00:12:06.239 --> 00:12:11.456
Well, in the vicinity of zero...

00:12:11.456 --> 00:12:15.326
It's more like a parabola...

00:12:15.326 --> 00:12:17.933
Beyond that, something incomprehensible...

00:12:17.933 --> 00:12:22.970
And this incomprehensible thing remains stable when m changes.

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:32.184
So, this part needs to be approximated with components to which we don't have to add a multiplier m1.

00:12:32.184 --> 00:12:38.556
I denote m1 like this.

00:12:38.556 --> 00:12:44.505
And at larger scales, the dependence on m is felt.

00:12:44.505 --> 00:12:51.612
You move m, and the numbers on the "y" axis change.

00:12:51.612 --> 00:12:53.059
And here, you can still move.

00:12:53.059 --> 00:12:55.442
Here, you can find something further...

00:12:55.442 --> 00:13:00.000
Some steps have been taken here. It doesn't matter which ones.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:07.375
Let's put a comma and write, for example, xp^6.

00:13:09.564 --> 00:13:11.564
There it is.

00:13:16.337 --> 00:13:20.000
What do we need to do here... Let's try dividing by m1.

00:13:23.416 --> 00:13:25.634
That's not fair. I guessed.

00:13:25.634 --> 00:13:28.499
I just guessed. It happens sometimes.

00:13:30.513 --> 00:13:36.618
And that's the complexity of this whole story.

00:13:36.618 --> 00:13:46.807
You need to have experience and quickly guess, otherwise the endeavor turns into tedious hours of trial and error.

00:13:46.807 --> 00:13:50.716
Guessed it! (x^6)/m1

00:13:50.716 --> 00:13:57.258
And now, when I subtract, it will be easier for me to deal with these oscillations.

00:13:57.258 --> 00:14:00.692
This component was found in a similar way.

00:14:00.692 --> 00:14:01.630
You understand, right?

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:04.976
I remove the comma, write a minus sign.

00:14:04.976 --> 00:14:06.792
Slap!

00:14:06.792 --> 00:14:09.263
Again, I go to a larger scale,

00:14:09.263 --> 00:14:10.858
I see these oscillations.

00:14:10.858 --> 00:14:12.146
Great.

00:14:12.146 --> 00:14:15.766
I currently like xm to be around 70.

00:14:15.766 --> 00:14:17.492
I want to act here.

00:14:17.492 --> 00:14:21.101
And I'm quite content with m = 80.

00:14:21.101 --> 00:14:25.104
I want to live on this x scale and with this m.

00:14:25.104 --> 00:14:28.919
I write a comma. xp to some power...

00:14:28.919 --> 00:14:32.084
xp = x/Pi.

00:14:32.084 --> 00:14:36.963
Sinp = Sin[x]/Pi.

00:14:36.963 --> 00:14:40.957
Simply during all these games, it was discovered that

00:14:40.957 --> 00:14:49.087
if you take x, then the next step will require dividing by 3. Something similar to 3.

00:14:49.087 --> 00:14:54.185
If you take x^2, then later you'll want to divide by Pi^2.

00:14:54.185 --> 00:14:55.792
So, x always goes with Pi.

00:14:55.792 --> 00:14:57.869
The same was noticed about Sin[x].

00:14:57.869 --> 00:15:05.713
If you subtract it, the multiplier to it contains 1/Pi.

00:15:05.713 --> 00:15:07.511
I missed this step. Sorry.

00:15:07.511 --> 00:15:11.295
Otherwise, the video would be too long.

00:15:11.295 --> 00:15:14.696
And not everyone will watch this video until the end.

00:15:14.696 --> 00:15:20.000
I think I will lose a significant portion of the audience here.

00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:25.054
But guys, don't get lost. This is very important!

00:15:25.054 --> 00:15:28.313
We will reach the zeta function.

00:15:28.313 --> 00:15:31.889
We started with a simple idea of approximating the sine with hyperbolas.

00:15:31.889 --> 00:15:41.045
And we will get to the zeta function in a matter of hours...

00:15:43.278 --> 00:15:44.209
So... what I want...

00:15:44.209 --> 00:15:52.132
I want to create oscillations with increasing amplitude.

00:15:52.132 --> 00:15:58.982
Let's assume the amplitude grows like x^4.

00:15:58.982 --> 00:16:00.173
Let's check...

00:16:04.508 --> 00:16:10.537
Hmm, some kind of incorrect artifact appears here.

00:16:10.537 --> 00:16:12.263
Mistaken one.

00:16:12.263 --> 00:16:15.171
Clearly a mistaken artifact.

00:16:16.616 --> 00:16:18.863
Alright...

00:16:23.986 --> 00:16:30.679
It feels ike the amplitude increases with a greater degree.

00:16:34.007 --> 00:16:37.979
Here I'm in phase,

00:16:37.979 --> 00:16:39.180
and here in antiphase.

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:41.749
So I guessed the parity wrong.

00:16:41.749 --> 00:16:44.833
Let's try the 5th power.

00:16:44.833 --> 00:16:47.031
Maybe it's actually the 3rd power.

00:16:47.031 --> 00:16:49.051
Yes!

00:16:49.051 --> 00:16:52.441
That's closer to the truth.

00:16:52.441 --> 00:16:56.262
Although I'm not certain.

00:16:58.495 --> 00:17:00.622
Not sure...

00:17:00.622 --> 00:17:08.333
Sines are better to look for in the region of small x values.

00:17:08.333 --> 00:17:10.620
Well, how can we understand it here...

00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:16.638
The power function that is hidden here interferes with the fitting.

00:17:19.353 --> 00:17:22.277
Let me try writing 2 here.

00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:27.418
Listen... maybe it's actually 2.

00:17:29.213 --> 00:17:31.128
Let's subtract 2.

00:17:31.128 --> 00:17:34.868
We can write "a" here...

00:17:34.868 --> 00:17:36.638
By the way, I added "a" here.

00:17:36.638 --> 00:17:38.113
...and try different values of "a".

00:17:38.113 --> 00:17:43.441
So, "a" is likely to be equal to one.

00:17:43.441 --> 00:17:45.561
And we'll leave the fitting of "a" for later.

00:17:45.561 --> 00:17:48.218
For now, let's subtract,

00:17:48.218 --> 00:17:51.521
to see this thing here.

00:17:51.521 --> 00:17:57.626
This is a parabola,

00:17:57.626 --> 00:18:02.067
which doesn't change its scale when m changes,

00:18:02.067 --> 00:18:06.782
but it changes over time...

00:18:11.249 --> 00:18:13.970
Let's give it a go...

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:17.216
xp^2

00:18:19.624 --> 00:18:23.213
With a minus sign.

00:18:23.213 --> 00:18:24.764
And it's not enough...

00:18:24.764 --> 00:18:27.322
Let's add a two.

00:18:27.322 --> 00:18:29.224
Still not enough...

00:18:30.844 --> 00:18:32.264
We're short on twos.

00:18:32.264 --> 00:18:35.654
Let's try a three.

00:18:35.654 --> 00:18:36.603
Oh!

00:18:36.603 --> 00:18:38.285
That looks like the truth.

00:18:38.285 --> 00:18:43.514
Very, very close to the truth.

00:18:43.514 --> 00:18:44.539
Yes! See?

00:18:44.539 --> 00:18:49.648
We have a parabola, and there's a sine wave oscillating around it.

00:18:49.648 --> 00:18:53.263
Excellent!

00:18:55.015 --> 00:18:57.148
Remove the comma.

00:18:57.148 --> 00:18:59.181
Change minus to plus.

00:18:59.181 --> 00:19:01.564
And so on...

00:19:01.564 --> 00:19:04.472
All of this can be unraveled in reverse.

00:19:04.472 --> 00:19:07.873
So we have some approximation here.

00:19:07.873 --> 00:19:09.687
We highlight, we copy,

00:19:09.687 --> 00:19:12.902
and we set it equal to zero.

00:19:12.902 --> 00:19:17.936
