00:22:53.896 --> 00:23:00.877
There seems to be some power visible, but it's already difficult. Really difficult.

00:23:00.877 --> 00:23:05.885
Moreover, as you approach small values of x,

00:23:05.885 --> 00:23:19.446
here you can see that we have reached a point where the floating-point precision is not enough.

00:23:19.446 --> 00:23:23.843
10^-16. We have already reached this limit.

00:23:23.843 --> 00:23:28.941
Here we are already living at the level of approximation of 10^-16.

00:23:28.941 --> 00:23:37.893
When you see such pictures with noise, it is a clear sign that it's time to stop.

00:23:37.893 --> 00:23:40.493
You can stop acting like a dumb programmer-engineer.

00:23:40.493 --> 00:23:44.945
You can start thinking.

00:23:44.945 --> 00:23:47.686
Moreover, this picture around zero,

00:23:47.686 --> 00:23:50.219
it looks like this.

00:23:50.219 --> 00:23:53.828
And here some sharp angle is observed,

00:23:53.828 --> 00:24:05.757
This is the moment when the story with m1, which is equal to m(m+1), ends,

00:24:05.757 --> 00:24:07.527
and it starts...

00:24:07.527 --> 00:24:12.801
Nothing starts. There is a deadlock. It becomes very difficult to move forward.

00:24:12.801 --> 00:24:20.570
If we ask Mathematica to give us an answer: what do we get if we set our approximation to zero?

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:22.996
As if we found an absolutely accurate approximation.

00:24:22.996 --> 00:24:33.272
If the error were not equal to this nonsense, but if it were equal to zero, what would the coveted "y" be?

00:24:33.272 --> 00:24:35.091
This is what it would be.

00:24:35.091 --> 00:24:41.503
And if we group the terms into these pieces,

00:24:41.503 --> 00:24:47.258
then we get the following approximation formula.

00:24:47.258 --> 00:24:52.324
We approximate Sin[x] with these poles.

00:24:52.324 --> 00:24:53.248
Here we go...

00:24:53.248 --> 00:25:02.430
And of course, after an hour of contemplation, anyone can guess that...

00:25:02.430 --> 00:25:04.594
I won't say the word "obviously."

00:25:04.594 --> 00:25:10.305
...that actually we should have divided the sine by this expression.

00:25:11.794 --> 00:25:14.498
Because these popping up sines here...

00:25:14.498 --> 00:25:19.252
First of all, it's silly. We are looking for an approximation of the sine, and somehow in this approximation, we have a sine.

00:25:19.252 --> 00:25:22.435
We don't solve the sine approximation problem like this.

00:25:22.435 --> 00:25:23.292
We want to get rid of them.

00:25:23.292 --> 00:25:25.292
Where did these sines come from?

00:25:25.292 --> 00:25:30.000
They appeared here because of this division.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:31.241
Here's what we have here.

00:25:31.241 --> 00:25:35.737
If we simplify the picture, here's what we have here.

00:25:35.737 --> 00:25:40.234
And the thought arises: what if we had to act differently?

00:25:40.234 --> 00:25:48.660
Take our approximating function "a" and the original sine function

00:25:48.660 --> 00:25:52.548
and consider this expression.

00:25:52.548 --> 00:25:56.945
Because if this expression equals something simple,

00:25:56.945 --> 00:26:03.575
where there is no such power series of sines,

00:26:03.575 --> 00:26:10.000
then, if this statement is true, then 1/asin...

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:12.583
we move -1 to the other side.

00:26:12.583 --> 00:26:19.356
We get that 1/asin will be equal to this.

00:26:19.356 --> 00:26:25.823
And we take this and consider it as "p".

00:26:25.823 --> 00:26:34.829
and we find that 1/(1-p) is the formula of an infinite geometric progression.

00:26:34.829 --> 00:26:40.779
And these sine powers pop up.

00:26:40.779 --> 00:26:44.206
Intuition, of course, is complex here.

00:26:44.206 --> 00:26:48.297
But it exists.

00:26:50.487 --> 00:26:56.446
And a person who doesn't know the correct answer could actually turn around here

00:26:56.446 --> 00:26:59.015
and choose this other path.

00:26:59.015 --> 00:27:00.538
We were dealing with this,

00:27:00.538 --> 00:27:03.107
now we'll be dealing with this.

00:27:03.107 --> 00:27:07.156
We go through the same process.

00:27:07.156 --> 00:27:09.627
We play the same game.

00:27:09.627 --> 00:27:18.141
And here we manage to achieve a greater number of m1

00:27:18.141 --> 00:27:22.506
and reach this pattern.

00:27:22.506 --> 00:27:32.085
Of course, I would very much like to show you all these steps, but no. There's not enough time.

00:27:32.085 --> 00:27:34.194
Some interesting moments

00:27:34.194 --> 00:27:38.252
Here I dare to divide by (x^2 - p).

00:27:38.252 --> 00:27:40.000
Yes, it's an interesting moment.

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:42.340
Maybe it's worth showing.

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:44.340
(No, it's not)

00:27:45.873 --> 00:27:52.470
As a result, we get this approximation.

00:27:52.470 --> 00:27:58.761
A long polynomial in which there is no Sin[x] and Sin[x] powers.

00:27:58.761 --> 00:28:02.250
Let's remember this as a certain answer.

00:28:02.250 --> 00:28:03.319
Alright.

00:28:03.319 --> 00:28:10.000
Here, a different approach is chosen, where I didn't divide by (x^2 - Pi^2),

00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:12.820
but raised this parabola by one,

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:15.728
then subtracted this parabola.

00:28:15.728 --> 00:28:18.504
An essentially equivalent option.

00:28:18.504 --> 00:28:25.485
And here's what we end up with when we bring this to the end.

00:28:25.485 --> 00:28:29.795
The last term is quite debatable.

00:28:29.795 --> 00:28:35.167
You can take a look at this option, for example.

00:28:35.167 --> 00:28:40.447
I just wrote it quite roughly.

00:28:40.447 --> 00:28:44.071
10 and 45.

00:28:44.071 --> 00:28:45.272
Maybe it's not 45.

00:28:45.272 --> 00:28:46.823
Maybe it's 44.

00:28:46.823 --> 00:28:52.004
You can't see it just by looking.

00:28:52.004 --> 00:28:58.892
But you won't believe it, 45 is actually the correct answer, as it turns out later.

00:28:58.892 --> 00:29:04.180
So I saw the component x in some large power,

00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:08.971
picked four and a multiplier of 45,

00:29:08.971 --> 00:29:13.366
and then subtracted.

00:29:13.366 --> 00:29:19.106
And here it's already difficult to proceed because as "m" increases...

00:29:19.106 --> 00:29:22.332
And I want to increase "m".

00:29:22.332 --> 00:29:27.649
As "m" increases, we already have some noise.

00:29:27.649 --> 00:29:36.267
And we've reached a point where machine precision no longer allows us to move further.

00:29:36.267 --> 00:29:39.304
We calculate the answer in this variant.

00:29:39.304 --> 00:29:41.142
Let's remember it.

00:29:41.142 --> 00:29:46.256
We ask Mathematica how much these two variants I found differ,

00:29:46.256 --> 00:29:49.602
with division by x^2 - Pi^2 and without division.

00:29:49.602 --> 00:29:51.778
They don't differ that much.

00:29:51.778 --> 00:29:54.993
In the second one, I managed to reach 45.

00:29:54.993 --> 00:29:57.726
You can see that the difference...

00:29:57.726 --> 00:30:05.320
Don't count the one, maybe I wrote it unnecessarily.

00:30:07.159 --> 00:30:10.803
Here we have a fourth power in the denominator of m1 everywhere.

00:30:10.803 --> 00:30:15.048
So it's actually an eighth power of m.

00:30:15.048 --> 00:30:17.650
And here things are not so straightforward.

00:30:17.650 --> 00:30:20.689
Yes, in the initial approximation, I used the letter "b".

00:30:20.689 --> 00:30:25.831
And, by the way, if b equals 9, then this pair vanishes.

00:30:25.831 --> 00:30:29.055
And if b equals 45, then this pair vanishes.

00:30:29.055 --> 00:30:34.049
That's why in that approximation, I couldn't decide on b.

00:30:34.049 --> 00:30:40.402
One component vanishes with one b, and the other with another.

00:30:40.402 --> 00:30:45.905
Therefore, the variant with division by (xp^2) - 1 is not quite correct.

00:30:45.905 --> 00:30:48.766
Division seems to be bad in general.

00:30:48.766 --> 00:30:50.580
So we need subtraction.

00:30:50.580 --> 00:30:52.791
As this specific problem shows,

00:30:52.791 --> 00:30:56.969
we need to do subtraction, not division.

00:30:56.969 --> 00:30:58.637
Okay.

00:31:01.571 --> 00:31:11.094
I wrote a rule here to eliminate all higher powers of m. Higher than 4.

00:31:11.094 --> 00:31:19.164
And precisely at this level, these two approximations coincide.

00:31:19.164 --> 00:31:23.766
But in the approximation up to the next fourth power.

00:31:23.766 --> 00:31:27.375
Here we see 45, 10

00:31:27.375 --> 00:31:29.678
6, 3, 13

00:31:29.678 --> 00:31:32.805
Here we already have interesting numbers different from one.

00:31:32.805 --> 00:31:37.378
By the way, it turned out that they all have only a numerator.

00:31:37.378 --> 00:31:40.831
Integers, integer coefficients.

00:31:40.831 --> 00:31:42.284
Why?

00:31:43.598 --> 00:31:45.598
Well...

00:31:47.525 --> 00:31:50.994
Let's go back to the number m.

00:31:50.994 --> 00:31:55.610
Here I replaced our gotten answer.

00:31:55.610 --> 00:31:59.659
In this answer, I replaced m1 with m(m+1)

00:31:59.659 --> 00:32:05.282
and asked to expand this expression in terms of powers of m.

00:32:05.282 --> 00:32:12.769
And here, too, we got integer values for all coefficients.

00:32:12.769 --> 00:32:18.410
As I said, everything seems reliable up to m^8.

00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:20.814
Essentially, we got such an approximation.

00:32:20.814 --> 00:32:23.898
1 minus Sin multiplied by the sum

00:32:23.898 --> 00:32:28.689
equals xSin/(Pi^2) multiplied by this polynomial,

00:32:28.689 --> 00:32:30.733
which you see in front of you.

00:32:30.733 --> 00:32:35.568
The polynomial starts with the fourth power of m.

00:32:35.568 --> 00:32:40.612
Well, previously it was just 1/(m1^2)

00:32:43.152 --> 00:32:47.389
And if we express the sine from this, here's what we get

00:32:49.053 --> 00:32:54.257
Let's see what happens if we return to the old idea.

00:32:54.257 --> 00:32:58.260
If we really divide 1/gg by sine. ("gg" is the summ)

00:32:58.260 --> 00:33:00.219
What will happen then?

00:33:00.219 --> 00:33:04.181
Then we get this expression.

00:33:04.181 --> 00:33:08.330
Here I divided by Pi^2 mentally

00:33:08.330 --> 00:33:14.228
and xsin also mentally contains division by Pi^2.

00:33:14.228 --> 00:33:22.097
xsin - it's Sin[x]/Pi^2 and also multiplied by x.

00:33:22.097 --> 00:33:26.644
So, this is what we get.

00:33:26.644 --> 00:33:32.837
That is, this is what we could have got in the first incorrect variant, if we had continued.

00:33:32.837 --> 00:33:35.854
But here we have m's, and we need to get m1.

00:33:35.854 --> 00:33:37.335
Well...

00:33:37.335 --> 00:33:40.000
Let's go back to m1 like this.

00:33:40.000 --> 00:33:43.550
Expressing m through m1 using the following formula.

00:33:43.550 --> 00:33:48.261
And then we ask to expand in terms of powers of m1 up to the fourth power.

00:33:48.261 --> 00:33:50.831
And we get something.

00:33:50.831 --> 00:33:57.885
Here we see exactly these components: 1, 2, 8, 13.

00:33:57.885 --> 00:34:00.856
Let's remember the result we had.

00:34:00.856 --> 00:34:03.852
Where is our beloved result.

00:34:05.166 --> 00:34:06.685
Here it is.

00:34:06.685 --> 00:34:08.272
I didn't execute this.

00:34:08.272 --> 00:34:12.526
The result we got by moving in the wrong path.

00:34:12.526 --> 00:34:15.661
And let's compare what we get from this formula

00:34:15.661 --> 00:34:18.362
