00:37:29.610 --> 00:37:33.745
Let's have a look.

00:37:33.745 --> 00:37:37.573
So, the blue plot

00:37:37.573 --> 00:37:40.203
is this variant.

00:37:40.203 --> 00:37:45.257
This is not that blue plot. This is the first approximation, where we take m1.

00:37:45.257 --> 00:37:50.000
Then the yellow plot appears.

00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:55.536
We took m1^3.

00:37:55.536 --> 00:38:00.548
The old approach.

00:38:00.548 --> 00:38:06.846
This is the new approach. Also m1^3.

00:38:06.846 --> 00:38:09.570
Here it is in green. The new approach,

00:38:09.570 --> 00:38:11.315
where we flipped the fractions.

00:38:11.315 --> 00:38:13.887
It works better.

00:38:13.887 --> 00:38:17.426
And the next approach: we take m1^3 here, and here we also have m1,

00:38:17.426 --> 00:38:21.148
m^4 approximation.

00:38:21.148 --> 00:38:23.114
This is the red plot.

00:38:23.114 --> 00:38:25.629
But there is also the violet one.

00:38:25.629 --> 00:38:34.449
The violet one is simply the approximation of the Taylor series taking m terms.

00:38:34.449 --> 00:38:44.145
Actually, I chose two m terms here because half of the terms in the sine expansion are zeros.

00:38:44.145 --> 00:38:48.130
So, it turns out that the Taylor series approximation works best here.

00:38:48.130 --> 00:38:55.768
That is, we didn't find any new, more efficient way to calculate sine.

00:38:55.768 --> 00:39:01.775
Although our method has its advantages. I'll show you what they are now.

00:39:01.775 --> 00:39:10.000
The Taylor series in the form of this plot goes to infinity abruptly,

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:18.689
while our approximations work over a large range of x values.

00:39:18.689 --> 00:39:20.848
See, right?

00:39:22.556 --> 00:39:25.420
Here is our red plot.

00:39:25.420 --> 00:39:31.396
It also has a tendency, of course. It works particularly well in the vicinity of small x values.

00:39:31.396 --> 00:39:39.212
But it works quite well around x = 20, unlike the Taylor expansion.

00:39:41.533 --> 00:39:43.583
Moving on...

00:39:43.583 --> 00:39:48.235
Further, we can find the formula for the number Pi.

00:39:48.235 --> 00:39:51.364
