00:39:51.364 --> 00:39:54.846
Here we got our approximation, subtract it from the sine.

00:39:54.846 --> 00:40:00.225
Set it equal to zero and replace x with (Pi^2)/6.

00:40:01.845 --> 00:40:05.189
Then replace Pi with pix.

00:40:05.189 --> 00:40:10.725
This expression here (/.), and then the rule in curly braces,

00:40:10.725 --> 00:40:19.142
they effectively replace what appears to the left of the rule with what appears to the right of the rule.

00:40:19.142 --> 00:40:29.402
And we get that Pi is expressed from this formula because,

00:40:29.402 --> 00:40:36.628
when we substitute x = (Pi^2)/6, because x is always paired with division by Pi,

00:40:36.628 --> 00:40:39.825
we simply get 1/6 raised to some power.

00:40:39.825 --> 00:40:41.419
Here it is (1/6)^4,

00:40:41.419 --> 00:40:44.470
and here (1/6)^2.

00:40:44.470 --> 00:40:45.857
So...

00:40:45.857 --> 00:40:50.265
Anyway, check for yourself.

00:40:50.265 --> 00:40:54.093
It turns out to be a simple linear equation for Pi.

00:40:54.093 --> 00:40:56.750
Well, Pi can be considered as x.

00:40:56.750 --> 00:40:59.221
And here's what we get.

00:40:59.221 --> 00:41:00.785
Wonderful!

00:41:00.785 --> 00:41:09.291
Let's fix this approximation as the function PiX and draw it.

00:41:11.875 --> 00:41:21.237
Calculate PiX here for different values of m.

00:41:21.237 --> 00:41:27.948
And we get such fractions.

00:41:27.948 --> 00:41:30.637
They are all close to Pi. Very close.

00:41:30.637 --> 00:41:34.990
Let's look at the difference between this and Pi.

00:41:34.990 --> 00:41:38.435
Simply -Pi.

00:41:38.435 --> 00:41:42.745
And you can see that they go to 10^-14, 10^-15.

00:41:42.745 --> 00:41:44.489
Let's draw the plot of errors.

00:41:44.489 --> 00:41:47.891
But it's better to draw not the errors themselves, but the logarithms of them.

00:41:47.891 --> 00:41:51.588
Here I take the absolute values of these errors,

00:41:51.588 --> 00:42:00.419
decimal logarithm, to understand how many correct digits of Pi we know.

00:42:00.419 --> 00:42:03.283
For m equal to ten, we know...

00:42:03.283 --> 00:42:06.848
Well.. some amount. 13 decimal places.

00:42:06.848 --> 00:42:10.687
I sum up 15 terms

00:42:10.687 --> 00:42:12.182
right here.

00:42:12.182 --> 00:42:15.233
And then I compute this formula.

00:42:15.233 --> 00:42:18.853
We have 15 digits of accuracy for the number Pi.

00:42:18.853 --> 00:42:29.130
And with 20 terms, we have -16. The logarithm of the error.

00:42:29.130 --> 00:42:36.461
So, we got a formula for approximating the sine function.

00:42:39.701 --> 00:42:42.041
What is its accuracy?

00:42:42.041 --> 00:42:43.840
We don't have a clear answer.

00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:48.281
We know the experimental error is 10^-13 for m = 10.

00:42:48.281 --> 00:42:53.247
That's not for Pi, but for the sine function.

00:42:53.247 --> 00:43:00.000
Somehow, m(m+1) appeared here. What is it?

00:43:01.839 --> 00:43:03.869
It's unclear for now.

00:43:05.796 --> 00:43:08.726
Why are the coefficients in this expansion integers?

00:43:08.726 --> 00:43:11.182
I'll ask this question again. It's an important question.

00:43:11.182 --> 00:43:17.274
When you see integer coefficients,

00:43:17.274 --> 00:43:20.620
it's likely some combinatorial object.

00:43:20.620 --> 00:43:24.842
That is, the quantity of some things.

00:43:24.842 --> 00:43:29.821
By the way, some Fibonacci numbers. 1, 2, 3, 8

00:43:29.821 --> 00:43:33.318
6 is not a Fibonacci number, but still.

00:43:33.318 --> 00:43:39.667
Is there any way to guess? To find some patterns in these numbers and guess what's next.

00:43:39.667 --> 00:43:43.153
Well.. I'll say right away: it's unlikely.

00:43:43.153 --> 00:43:45.269
It's difficult to guess.

00:43:45.269 --> 00:43:49.579
Mathematica knows the truth, what this sum equals.

00:43:49.579 --> 00:43:50.648
No, actually, it doesn't know.

00:43:50.648 --> 00:43:53.261
It makes an error here...

00:43:53.261 --> 00:43:55.345
You input this sum and it says:

00:43:55.345 --> 00:44:02.731
I have these nice smooth analytical functions somehow programmed inside.

00:44:02.731 --> 00:44:07.181
And these functions...

00:44:07.181 --> 00:44:10.320
You can check the help...

00:44:12.597 --> 00:44:19.383
It says it's just a sum of (k+a)^s, where k varies from 0 to infinity.

00:44:19.383 --> 00:44:24.437
Well, it turns out that if we take, for example, a = -10,

00:44:24.437 --> 00:44:26.820
and then take a = 10,

00:44:26.820 --> 00:44:33.188
and then subtract this function Φ, where a = -10 and a = 10,

00:44:33.188 --> 00:44:34.750
we get a finite sum.

00:44:34.750 --> 00:44:36.389
Well, indeed.

00:44:36.389 --> 00:44:38.290
But what does "s" equal?

00:44:38.290 --> 00:44:40.320
One.

00:44:40.320 --> 00:44:41.305
Well, yes, that's what he writes.

00:44:41.305 --> 00:44:43.269
"s" equals one,

00:44:43.269 --> 00:44:48.356
and "a" is some complex expression, in short, it wrote.

00:44:48.356 --> 00:44:50.984
We could trust Mathematica,

00:44:50.984 --> 00:44:56.838
but no. Here there is actually some error.

00:44:56.838 --> 00:44:58.451
Slap!

00:44:59.765 --> 00:45:01.692
If we subtract this

00:45:01.692 --> 00:45:07.327
from this. That is, add 0.811, we get 2.

00:45:07.327 --> 00:45:12.663
This is the true value of the sum. I just plugged in a specific value here.

00:45:12.663 --> 00:45:23.978
And here, it used it's great intellect to turn this sum into a specific expression of an analytical function,

00:45:23.978 --> 00:45:28.080
and then he plugged it in and got a different answer.

00:45:28.080 --> 00:45:37.119
This is because this function has poles along the path from -20 to 20,

00:45:37.119 --> 00:45:43.717
and you need to be very careful about how you bypass these poles, from which side.

00:45:43.717 --> 00:45:46.083
We went too far, in short.

00:45:46.083 --> 00:45:52.608
And miracle of miracles! Mathematica knows the series expansion of these functions.

00:45:52.608 --> 00:45:56.024
And you can simply ask it for the answer.

00:45:56.024 --> 00:46:02.304
This function G, which we previously called gg of m.

00:46:02.304 --> 00:46:06.858
And I ask, what is the expansion in terms of x?

00:46:06.858 --> 00:46:09.579
It knows the expansion in terms of x.

00:46:09.579 --> 00:46:11.163
Oh my God! Pi squared is here.

00:46:11.163 --> 00:46:15.473
It knows everything. Everything has already been calculated before us.

00:46:15.473 --> 00:46:19.649
All these formulas were found a long time ago.

00:46:22.671 --> 00:46:28.070
But we need to expand the function G

00:46:28.070 --> 00:46:30.814
(or rather, this expression here)

00:46:30.814 --> 00:46:35.211
in terms of powers of x and powers of 1/m.

00:46:35.211 --> 00:46:37.625
How do we do that?

00:46:37.625 --> 00:46:41.917
Well, from here on, it's just about looking and enjoying.

00:46:41.917 --> 00:46:44.073
Here...

00:46:44.073 --> 00:46:49.478
it showed his intestines. That he knows all about it.

00:46:49.478 --> 00:46:55.567
Here, I allow the computation to be done correctly.

00:46:55.567 --> 00:47:01.140
Please expand it as a series around zero up to x^8.

00:47:01.140 --> 00:47:03.929
And in the vicinity of n, which is infinity,

00:47:03.929 --> 00:47:07.987
but this means that I want to expand it as a series in 1/m.

00:47:07.987 --> 00:47:13.593
I also want to get the expansion up to 1/m^8.

00:47:13.593 --> 00:47:17.411
He starts writing zeta functions here.

00:47:17.411 --> 00:47:20.232
Here we have finally reached them.

00:47:20.232 --> 00:47:24.284
And I tell him that

00:47:24.284 --> 00:47:31.019
it can be simplified by removing the option he added.

00:47:31.019 --> 00:47:39.641
Then I have to ask him to replace m with 1/u and divide it by 2 moreover.

00:47:39.641 --> 00:47:40.448
Why?

00:47:40.448 --> 00:47:43.750
I remind you that the expansion depended on the parity of m,

00:47:43.750 --> 00:47:46.133
so here I wrote 2m,

00:47:46.133 --> 00:47:47.775
and m belongs to Integers.

00:47:47.775 --> 00:47:51.795
You can write it like this. That's how smart it is.

00:47:51.795 --> 00:47:55.604
When you have an expression that depends on whether

00:47:55.604 --> 00:47:58.348
m is an integer or not, what parity m has,

00:47:58.348 --> 00:48:05.767
you surround that expression with Assuming and write a list of your assumptions.

00:48:05.767 --> 00:48:13.110
Your knowledge of the variables involved in the expression here.

00:48:13.110 --> 00:48:18.346
That's how you can manipulate it to get the answer.

00:48:18.346 --> 00:48:23.174
And this answer is quite complex.

00:48:23.174 --> 00:48:25.408
It's a complicated one.

00:48:25.408 --> 00:48:32.400
But let me rather show you this.

00:48:32.400 --> 00:48:36.009
It turns out to be a quite complicated answer.

00:48:36.009 --> 00:48:40.000
And this answer involves u.

00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:48.776
And we need to ask Mathematica for this series in terms of x (powers of x)

00:48:48.776 --> 00:48:54.498
to apply another expansion series in terms of u.

00:48:57.476 --> 00:49:03.774
This was done, and we got this answer.

00:49:08.854 --> 00:49:12.880
There is a long pause here because...

00:49:14.150 --> 00:49:19.799
Of course, it's unclear how we can reach all of this.

00:49:19.799 --> 00:49:23.901
Up to this code.

00:49:23.901 --> 00:49:28.054
It's unclear how all this knowledge was built into Mathematica.

00:49:31.908 --> 00:49:33.424
Well... what can be done here...

00:49:34.869 --> 00:49:40.587
There are gaps in my videos...

00:49:45.141 --> 00:49:51.093
It's inevitable, we have to accept it.

00:49:51.093 --> 00:50:00.798
By the way, the support team replied to me, confirming that there is indeed an error in the "summ" function,

00:50:00.798 --> 00:50:04.038
and it's quite amusing,

00:50:04.038 --> 00:50:06.946
that

00:50:06.946 --> 00:50:09.285
this sum

00:50:09.285 --> 00:50:16.715
is not automatically expanded into the correct analytical form.

00:50:19.211 --> 00:50:23.887
Let's compare the truth we got here with what we previously calculated

00:50:23.887 --> 00:50:26.349
when we were dealing with component selection.

00:50:26.349 --> 00:50:28.305
Here's what we had there.

00:50:28.305 --> 00:50:31.578
Well... actually, we had this, I remind you.

00:50:31.578 --> 00:50:37.377
1, 2, 8, 13, 3, 45... and our 10, these last two terms.

00:50:37.377 --> 00:50:42.018
And then we simply substituted m1 with m(m+1),

00:50:42.018 --> 00:50:45.540
expanded it in terms of 1/m

00:50:45.540 --> 00:50:47.803
and got this thing.

00:50:47.803 --> 00:50:50.679
With integer coefficients!

00:50:50.679 --> 00:50:53.423
The alarm goes off immediately!

00:50:54.999 --> 00:50:56.316
And what do we see?

00:50:56.316 --> 00:50:59.550
1, 1, 3, 17, 56...

00:50:59.550 --> 00:51:03.027
Hmm... something's wrong.

00:51:04.341 --> 00:51:06.963
1, 1, 1, 3, 17, 155...

00:51:06.963 --> 00:51:09.478
Well, that's already an incorrect term.

00:51:09.478 --> 00:51:10.503
It is clear.

00:51:10.503 --> 00:51:13.082
For x^2: 1, 2, 5, 28

00:51:13.082 --> 00:51:15.607
1, 2, 5, 28...

00:51:15.607 --> 00:51:18.001
92 for m^10.

00:51:18.001 --> 00:51:20.352
Well, in the 10th power, it's definitely wrong.

00:51:20.352 --> 00:51:23.523
The 8th is still correct.

00:51:23.523 --> 00:51:25.862
x^4

00:51:25.862 --> 00:51:27.512
as well.

00:51:27.512 --> 00:51:29.074
Great.

00:51:29.074 --> 00:51:37.719
So, using experimental methods, we correctly found everything up to the 8th power of 1/m.

00:51:37.719 --> 00:51:40.429
This was confirmed by Mathematica,

00:51:40.429 --> 00:51:43.991
which we trust,

00:51:43.991 --> 00:51:48.038
if we disregard these small errors.

00:51:48.038 --> 00:51:49.242
Alright.

00:51:50.425 --> 00:51:52.510
Let's start thinking!

00:51:52.510 --> 00:51:55.273
To be continued...

